2026-09-23: K10c/K10d source-bound diagnoses using real kernel APIs.

Original REDs are retained in the complete test_progs raw captures, not inferred
from a zero-exit mock: stacktrace_build_id fails compare_stack_ips on both
kernels; the GCC complete flight also gets zero bytes at its immediate read of
stream_arena_callback_fault. No original assertion or BPF program is patched.

K10c: bpf-stack-buildid-order.c loads small raw-BPF programs at the original
urandom_read_iter kprobe. Each calls the same two original helpers, with their
order reversed in the control. Both kernels produce exactly:
  get_stackid first: stack map 7 VALID / 0 IP; array 0 VALID / 7 IP; 224 bytes
  get_stack first:   stack map 0 VALID / 7 IP; array 7 VALID / 0 IP; 224 bytes
Frozen kernel/bpf/mmap_unlock_work.h explicitly defers mmap_read_unlock when
IRQs are disabled, using one per-CPU irq_work slot; the next lookup falls back
while that slot is busy. stackmap.c handles this by returning IP representations.
The original kprobe invokes these helpers consecutively but test_progs compares
all representation bytes. Its own NMI companion already avoids this comparison
for the analogous one-outstanding-translation boundary. This is a test/API
assumption mismatch, not permission to require two identical build-id snapshots.

K10d: bpf-stream-callback-completion.c loads the retained upstream stream.skel.h
without changing its BPF instructions. It selects the synchronous subprogram
fault and asynchronous timer fault separately, executes each original program
once, then observes the stream. The 100 x 10 ms polling bound is diagnostic-only:
  GCC synchronous: first/final 839 bytes, polls 0
  GCC callback:    first 0, final 888 bytes, polls 1
  Evo synchronous:first/final 961 bytes, polls 0
  Evo callback:   first/final 958 bytes, polls 0
Every nonempty record contains the expected unmapped-arena WRITE fault message.
helpers.c arms a soft hrtimer; test_loader.c reads immediately after program
return and requires >0 bytes, without observing callback completion. A zero
initial read is demonstrably not evidence that the callback produced no fault.
This explains the recorded reference failure; it is not an EvoCC semantic fix.

Both diagnostic programs: 4/4 assertions PASS per Image, both orderly shutdowns.
Unchanged original selected replay: test_progs -t stacktrace_build_id,stream_success
Both report 2/8 PASSED, 1 SKIPPED, 1 FAILED: stream_success 8/8, build-id equality
still FAIL, NMI variant SKIP on HVF. These counters are upstream counters, not
disjoint TAP populations. No full 970-program/test_progs receipt is rewritten.
K10 still requires the full original workload within 300 seconds and K12 config.

Build: sealed linux/arm64, network none, gcc-14 -O0 -g -Wall -Wextra -Werror.
The raw-BPF probe is static, -fno-omit-frame-pointer -Wl,--build-id=sha1.
The stream observer uses the retained selftests/bpf/tools/build/libbpf/libbpf.a,
its tools/include and stream.skel.h, with -lelf -lz -lzstd. The diagnostic payload
adds only its five inspected dynamic dependencies (libelf/libz/libc/libzstd/ld).
An initial missing u64 skeleton alias failed compilation, then a missing program
in an incompletely generated payload produced two real failed executions. A
static libelf attempt also failed to link its missing eu_search_tree symbols.
None is a PASS. The corrected alias and dynamic dependency closure produced
the complete 5.4 MiB payload below; no new disk or kernel clone was made.

